DESIGN CONSTRAINT found by reading both reduction loops -- the obvious implementation of this lever is NOT bit-exact, and the existing code already shows the way around it.

The cpg==2 backward needs four quantities per group: mean, rstd, sum0 (sum over channel 0's spatial run) and sum1. The forward currently computes only mean and rstd, and it accumulates the group sum as ONE sequential loop over the whole 1568-element group:

    for &v in xb { sum += v }

If the forward is naively extended to produce sum0 and sum1 by summing each half separately and then adding them (sum = sum0 + sum1), the association changes and 00000     0 -- hence mean, hence rstd, hence every output element -- changes in the last ULP. That is a silent behaviour change in the FORWARD, on the dominant f32 path, to make a BACKWARD faster.

The existing backward already solves this and the fix is to copy its shape exactly:

    for &v in x0 { sum += v; sum0 += v; }
    for &v in x1 { sum += v; sum1 += v; }

00000     0 still accumulates in the same sequential order as the forward's single loop (x0 then x1 IS xb in order), so it is bit-identical, while sum0/sum1 come along for free on data already loaded. So the forward must be split into per-half loops that keep a single running 00000     0, NOT into independent per-half sums.

Revised saving estimate: the backward's stats phase is two full reads of x (one for sum0/sum1/sum, one for vsum which needs mean first). Reuse removes BOTH, leaving only the write pass's single read plus its write -- 3 reads + 1 write becomes 1 read + 1 write.

TEST OBLIGATION, beyond gradients matching: assert the forward-saved (mean, rstd, sum0, sum1) are BIT-IDENTICAL to what group_norm_backward_scalar_f32 recomputes from the same x. Comparing only the resulting gradients would let a reassociated 00000     0 hide inside a tolerance.

