Every row I banked on 2026-08-15 used /data/projects/frankentorch/.venv (torch 2.12.0+cpu). The campaign pin is 2.12.1, which lives at /data/tmp/torchvenv-2121/bin/python (NEGATIVE_EVIDENCE item 17). A delta whose incumbent arm moved is not comparable, so those rows are vs-2.12.0 rows and the pinned board is still empty.

DELIVERABLE: one row on a NARROW op, measured against the pinned 2.12.1 arm, banked whatever it says. A certified LOSS counts; an undecidable lane does not.

GATE for banking, all of which the harness already prints, so nothing here is new machinery:
  - both A/A nulls PASS (point within +/-0.02 of 1.0 AND a calm CI) on the estimator the row is quoted under;
  - parity 'match';
  - both estimators agree on direction (the harness flags disagreement as unusable under either);
  - provenance: harness file, same_host, rch build WORKER, ELF digest, rounds, observed load, and the arm's self-reported PT_TORCH_VERSION -- which must read 2.12.1.

DO NOT bank from a single invocation: items 14b-14e measured single-invocation rows moving 5-9%, and my own banked floor had to be corrected from 1.07x to 1.006x after two extra replicates. Two clean invocations minimum, and quote the lowest confidence bound either produced rather than the better run's point estimate.

CANDIDATES, narrowest first: prelu_noshortcut (nine invocations of history on 2.12.0, so a 2.12.1 run is directly comparable in shape); max_pool3d_nopool (a LOSS, already certified twice -- by me on 2.12.0 at 2.85x and by frankentorch-y4nj9 on 2.12.1 at 2.74-2.99x, so a third reading has a known band to fall in).

NEGATIVE OUTCOME that must be recorded rather than retried away: if no lane clears the gate in two invocations at a workable load, record that and stop -- the answer is that this host cannot certify today, not that the number is whatever the least-bad run said.

Found while fixing frankentorch-3eq5b. NOT yet verified by running it — verify before fixing.

tensor_rrelu (crates/ft-api/src/lib.rs, ~line 22160) calls tensor_apply_function_f64_borrowed_inputs with NO dtype guard. Its siblings relu6 and selu BOTH guard first:

    let dt = self.tensor_dtype(input)?;
    if dt != DType::F64 { let in64 = self.tensor_to_dtype(input, DType::F64)?; ... }

and their comments state the reason outright: 'the f64 borrowed-inputs path errors on non-f64 storage' (frankentorch-x9yuq).

The tape-level helper apply_function_f64_borrowed_inputs (crates/ft-autograd/src/lib.rs ~9037) does two things that make f32 wrong:
  1. reads inputs via node.tensor.contiguous_values()  <- f64 accessor
  2. builds the output meta as TensorMeta::from_shape(output_shape, DType::F64, device)  <- output dtype HARDCODED to F64

So f32 rrelu either errors outright or silently returns an F64 tensor from an F32 input. torch preserves the input dtype. Same class as the asymmetric-dtype/f32-parity vein.

Decisive probe (add to ft-api tests):
    let x = s.tensor_variable_f32(vec![-1.0, 0.0, 1.0], vec![3], false)?;
    let o = s.tensor_rrelu(x, 0.125, 1.0/3.0)?;
    assert_eq!(s.tensor_dtype(o)?, DType::F32);

Fix shape if confirmed: mirror relu6/selu — upcast-compute-narrow guard at the top of tensor_rrelu, and keep the x > 0 compare that 3eq5b established (x == 0 takes the slope branch, verified vs torch 2.12.1+cpu).

Also worth checking in the same sweep: every OTHER caller of tensor_apply_function_f64_borrowed_inputs that lacks a dtype guard has the same latent defect. Grep for it rather than fixing rrelu alone.
